# Root-cause-first debugging (highest priority)

When anything breaks — type errors, failing tests, runtime bugs, lint violations — diagnose the underlying cause before changing code.

## Required approach
1. Reproduce and read the actual error; trace it to its origin (data source, type model, logic), not the line where it surfaced.
2. State the root cause explicitly before applying a fix.
3. Fix at the origin. If a bad value comes from the API layer, fix normalization there — not in the component that crashed.
4. After fixing, check for the same flawed pattern elsewhere in the codebase.

## Forbidden quick fixes
- `@ts-ignore`, casting to `any`/`as unknown as X` to silence type errors
- Optional chaining / default values that hide missing data instead of fixing why it's missing
- try/catch that swallows errors without handling them
- Disabling ESLint rules or React StrictMode to make warnings go away
- Retries, timeouts, or `setTimeout` hacks to paper over race conditions
- Deleting or skipping failing tests to make CI pass

## When a workaround is genuinely necessary
Only when the root cause is outside our control (e.g. upstream API bug). Then: isolate it in the adapter layer, comment why, and note the upstream cause.
